\begin{shaded}

\section*{Summary} A retrospective analysis of the Pember Fan monitoring data indicates that the species composition of the fan is changing. Grasses (both exotic and indigenous) are increasing in areal coverage while forbs are diminishing. At the same time the amount of bare ground is shrinking. There are also indications the fan is becoming more depauperate with indigenous biodiversity decreasing over time. \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical), an indigenous forb restricted to the southern half of the fan, is disappearing quickly, and global extinction appears imminent if its current trajectory continues.  As evidenced by this retrospective analysis, the current monitoring regime has proven sufficient for detecting changes in  community composition and areal coverage of the species of primary concern.  


\end{shaded}


\section*{Background}

The Pember Fan is an alluvial outwash surface in Lees Valley, North Canterbury, formed from gravels deposited where the Ashley/Rakahuri River emerges into the valley \citep{newell}. Much of the area is classified as a 'threatened land environment’. The fan supports a grassland which includes several nationally threatened and at-risk plants, most notably the nationally critical Lees Valley daisy (\emph{Brachyscome pinnata}), whose only known natural population occurs there and whose seeds, unlike most members of the Asteraceae family, are not wind dispersed \citep{newell}. Other threatened species known to inhabit the fan include \emph{Gingidia enysii}, \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} and \emph{Raoulia monroi}\footnote{Not present in the transect data}.

In 2018 the Department of Conservation established a monitoring programme based on 30 transects\footnote{Called Plots in the parent data set.} (each with 50 subplots) to monitor vegetative changes in this ecosystem through time \citep{newell}. The underlying purpose of this monitoring programme was to inform management. Key metrics focused on changes in:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Indigenous biodiversity
  \item Plant composition (especially threatened species) 
  \item Ground cover
\end{itemize}

The 30 transects which make up the monitoring programme consist of two types: (1)  ten closely spaced transects restricted to a recreational reserve, and (2) twenty more widely spaced transects found across a planned conservation covenant.

\section*{Purpose of current analysis}

It is hypothesised that the current grazing regime is insufficient to regulate the crowding out of native herbs by exotic grasses at the site. Going forward it was agreed that a two-stage analysis of the data would take place:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stage 1:} Would consist of a series of retrospective analyses to understand the trajectories at the site in respect of the three metrics previously identified. The deliverables were to include a short report that would inform a discussion with Richard Clayton and Ellen Cieraad regarding the direction of the next stage [this document].
  \item \textbf{Stage 2:} Based on the decisions undertaken at the end of Stage 1, the signals present in the data, and operational constraints, a series of statistical tools would be employed to identify the design parameters required to detect the effectiveness of a future management  intervention. On 24 October 2026 a video meeting was held in order to inform management conversations with the Lees Valley Station for the forthcoming season\footnote{Sandy Yong, Ellen Cieraad, and Nathan Whitmore were in attendance.}. The management recommendations were:
  
  
\begin{itemize}
  \item Graze the whole of the fan with cattle this spring (2026) to `reset' by reducing the thatch. 
  \item Split the fan in half along existing fencing lines to have more control over evenness of grazing over the whole of the fan (noting that \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} appears restricted to the southern portion of the fan, and that open grazing could result in this area being under grazed).
  \item Include a specific number of cattle in the recreation reserve to similarly drive down the thatch to be followed by sheep grazing in the autumn.
\end{itemize}
  

  


\end{itemize}




\section*{Analysis}

The analysis began with some simple proof-of-concept analyses involving indigenous biodiversity and sequentially introduced complexity as contextual understanding grew. Indigenous biodiversity was examined using generalised linear mixed-effect models (GLMMs), species composition was approached visually, bare ground and species coverage were assessed using cumulative link models (CLMMs).

All analyses were undertaken in program R version 4.4.2 \citep{rcode}, using a series of packages for increased functionality including: `AICcmodavg' \citep{AICcmodavg}, `glmmTMB' \citep{glmmTMB}, `ordinal' \citep{ordinal}, `sf' \citep{sf}, and the `tidyverse' \citep{tidyverse}. An information-theoretic approach (\emph{sensu} \citealt{burnham}) was used to rank candidate models in terms of parsimony (see Appendix for relevant model selection tables).


\subsection*{Indigenous biodiversity}

Initial exploratory modelling focused on indigenous biodiversity due to its high tangibility and suggested the area is becoming more depauperate over time (see Appendix). When more complex analyses were undertaken incorporating spatial elements it was revealed  that while the transects across the covenant area (CA plots) were well dispersed across the fan, the RR plots were tightly clustered within a reserve found on the southern edge of the fan but within the wider CA extent. Such tight spatial clustering typically presents a problem in terms of sampling bias and raises issues of spatial autocorrelation. Formal tests of the random errors via Moran's I test confirmed that there was some form of spatial autocorrelation. Initial models\footnote{At the suggestion of Ellen Cieraad I implemented a series of models which examined whether the response variable was associated with a decaying distance relationship with neighbouring observations i.e. via a \emph{exp(pos + 0 $\mid$ spatial\_group)}.} failed to converge raising the possibility there might be a directional gradient underpinning the random errors. 

Plotting the random errors associated with the top-ranked model suggested a spatial relationship running perpendicular to the Ashley River (Figure \ref{fig:spatial.signal}). Richard Clayton's suggestion that the relationship might be better described by the relationship with the fence line was then tested. Indeed, modelling revealed that there was likely some gradient influencing the biodiversity response associated with distance from the fence line (Figure \ref{fig:signal.corrected}). Inclusion of this term resulted in the random errors associated with plot diminishing substantially, and a new top-ranked model being established. 

The predictions from the new top-ranked model (\emph{Year * scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot/Subplot)}, AICc weight = 0.63) suggested that samples found closer to the fence line have higher indigenous biodiversity but are more likely to lose indigenous species at a faster rate than those located further away (Figure \ref{fig:distance.fence}).


