\subsection*{Bare ground cover}

Analyses of bare ground were conducted as cover classes via cumulative link mixed-effect models (which account for the non-independence of the subplots). Bare ground was also assessed as approximate proportions by transforming cover classes into proportions at the subplot level. While other cover classes on the fan appear to be stable, it is evident that bare ground appears to be disappearing regardless of metric (Figure \ref{fig:barecoverclass}, \ref{fig:bare}, \& \ref{fig:bgcover}). The top-ranked model was \emph{as.factor(Year) + (1 $\mid$ Plot) + (1 $\mid$ SubplotID)} (AICc weight = 0.73). The predictions from this model (Appendix Figure \ref{fig:barepred}) closely align with those observed (Figure \ref{fig:barecoverclass}). The second-ranked model replicated the variables of the first but with the inclusion of distance from fence, and indicated bare ground increased with distance from the fence. The stronger support for \emph{Year} as a factor rather than as a continuous variable suggests that bare ground cover varies year-to-year in a more idiosyncratic manner rather than simply a linear trend over time.


