\subsection*{Key species}

The three plant species present on the fan with the highest threat classification were singled out for modelling\footnote{I note that while the fan-leaved mat daisy \emph{Raoulia monroi} (Nationally Vulnerable) has been reported as being present in the valley it was not recorded in the current dataset.}. These were:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Lees Valley Daisy, \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical)
  \item Dryland sow thistle \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} (Nationally Vulnerable) 
    \item Limestone aniseed \emph{Gingidia enysii} (Nationally Endangered) 

\end{itemize}

As plant cover was estimated using ordered cover class categories, cumulative link models with random effects (CLMMs) were employed for modelling. 

\emph{Brachyscome pinnata} appears to be constrained to transects on the southern portion of the fan and appears to be fast disappearing (Figure \ref{fig:brapin}). Cumulative modelling revealed that the top-ranked model with unanimous support was \emph{as.factor(Year) + scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot) + (1 $\mid$ SubplotID)}, which revealed cover for this species is declining especially in the last three years, and there is a trend of higher coverage closer to the fence (although this remains non-significant). 

There is some evidence that \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} is declining through time (Figure \ref{fig:sonnov}; top-ranked model \emph{Year + (1 $\mid$ Plot) + (1 $\mid$ SubplotID}, AICc weight = 0.41)(see Appendix \ref{fig:sonchus.pred}). However, this is contested by the second-ranked model suggests the pattern might be solely attributable to distance from fence (i.e. \emph{scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot) + (1 $\mid$ SubplotID)}, AICc weight = 0.37).

\emph{Gingidia enysii} has only been found on two transects (once in a single subplot on R10 and in five subplots on RR3). Modelling was restricted to RR3 (hence no plot random effect was included) and the candidate model set was limited to a null model, a year effect, and a year-as-factor effect. The top-ranked model (\emph{1 + (1 $\mid$ SubplotID)}) suggested the presence of \emph{G. enysii} was best explained as a simple average (AIC weight = 0.68), while the second-ranked model (\emph{Year  + (1 $\mid$ Subplot)}  suggested that the cover class of the species was increasing year-on-year (AIC weight = 0.26) likely due to a proliferation at a single subplot (RR3 subplot 960).




