\appendix


\section*{Conclusions}

As evidenced by this retrospective analysis, the current monitoring regime has proven entirely adequate for assessing community and population trajectories on the Pember Fan and for this reason should be continued without change. The trajectories found in the data set align with the observations and assessments of DOC staff. Grasses (both exotic and indigenous) are increasing in areal coverage. At the same time the amount of bare ground is shrinking and there are indications the fan is becoming more depauperate over time. \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical), an indigenous forb restricted to the southern half of the fan, is disappearing quickly, and global extinction appears imminent if its current trajectory continues.  

In light of current management constraints, an experimental approach in terms of grazing has been assessed as impractical. However, localised experimental plots could be established on the fan, but outside the current transect lines to trial the effectiveness of vegetation control via controlled intervention around known \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} plants. Such an experiment should have the benefit of minimal impingement on farm activities while increasing understanding of effects of potential management interventions on presence and abundance of targetted herbaceous species.


